Chapter 10: The Lion King's Land

Chapter 10, Part 13: Unstained by Blood

---

The residence of Miklotov McMahon, a member of the Council of Elders, stood in the noble district of the royal capital Lugunica. Its subdued exterior reflected the temperament of its master, known for his austere and upright character.

Most nobles delighted in evening parties and banquets, frequently hosting such events to flaunt their power and influence. But Miklotov chose no such aristocratic conduct. Having distinguished himself as a civil official of the kingdom from his youth, the sage elder—whose abilities had been highly valued—devoted his life to the kingdom. As if to demonstrate his dedication, he never took a wife, grinding himself to the bone in service to the realm.

For that reason, ostentatious gaudiness had never once held sway over the McMahon estate in the noble district—at least, not until this day, this very moment.

The gruesome sight of countless lives cruelly cut down, scattered corpses wafting the scent of blood and death—this was the state of affairs that had brought Miklotov McMahon's life to its end as well.

And now—

"..."

Dumbfounded, faced with an unbelievable scene, Natsuki Subaru found himself frozen in place.

"——"

He had been invited, yet had encountered not a single living soul within the McMahon estate on his way here. Thirteen dead—likely the mansion's guards and servants—all cut down in a single stroke, their lives taken. He had confirmed each one before finally arriving at this innermost chamber.

Judging by the arranged desk and bookshelves, was this Miklotov's office?

A place steeped in history, where the sage elder had spent his days agonizing over the kingdom's future, wracking his brain and laboring to address its myriad problems—and there, against the far wall, Miklotov himself leaned.

With both legs stretched out and his back against the wall, he looked like a child, exhausted from play, who had fallen asleep. That description was, in its broad strokes, not wrong. The sage elder had fallen into slumber.

Draped in the severed flag of Lugunica, a slumber from which he would never awaken.

And beside the deceased sage elder—

"Lady Crusch...?"

There lay Miklotov, collapsed, his deeply wrinkled face with its eyes closed. And beside him, turning halfway to face Subaru, stood the person whose presence made Subaru's voice tremble pitifully.

Standing there was a beautiful woman with long green hair and amber eyes, clad in a dark navy military-style uniform—her left eye covered by a large eyepatch. It was Crusch Karsten, standing with the poised bearing Subaru knew so well.

"——"

At Subaru's faltering call, Crusch narrowed her lone amber eye. She readjusted her grip on the slender sword in her hand and, keeping her gaze sharp, parted her thin lips.

"A wind of fear blows. Natsuki Subaru—what is it you fear?"

"F-fear...? Fear? Me? That's..."

"In the first place, what business brings you here? Did you have some personal connection with Miklotov McMahon?"

"He summoned me, and I had things I wanted to ask him..."

"On this very day? I find that difficult to accept. What were these things you wished to ask about? Pertaining to the Royal Selection? Or perhaps some other secret discussion? I have not struck the mark, yet your agitation only adds to the wind. I will ask you once more: are you the man I know—the real one?"

"——!"

Overwhelmed by the torrent of accusations Crusch hurled at him, Subaru could not string together a proper reply.

Still unable to fully process the information before his eyes, he found Crusch's words—cutting in with razor-sharp eloquence—pressing down on him like a blade.

She continued to bore into him with her gaze, as if intent on cutting open his chest—

"That's quite enough."

"Yes, this is hardly a pleasant sight to behold."

Two figures, unable to stand by and watch the exchange between Subaru and Crusch, cut in from the side. One was Beatrice, here holding Subaru's hand. The other was Tiga, his companion.

Beatrice through the sensation of her small hand, and Tiga by stepping diagonally forward to block Crusch's line of sight—both pulled Subaru, who had been overwhelmed, back to his senses.

Thanks to them, he remembered to breathe. The cold sweat that belatedly burst forth cooled the heat that had gripped him.

In that interval, Tiga, his crescent-shaped curved blade in hand, faced Crusch. He lowered the brim of his hat, concealing his eyes. "Hey," he called out.

"You'd better be aware that this whole situation is ripe for misunderstandings, Duchess."

"Misunderstandings?"

"That's right—misunderstandings. After all, this many people have lost their lives, and a key figure of the kingdom is dead at the scene. If you're standing right there with a sword in hand, anyone would think it. That you, Duchess Crusch Karsten, are the one who killed everyone in this mansion."

Though he never abandoned his characteristic manner of speaking, it was a direct question. And it was precisely the fact that Subaru, too, wanted confirmed above all else.

"——"

Tiga was right.

In this atrocity that had occurred at the McMahon estate, the prime suspect for the slaughter of everyone, including its master Miklotov, was undeniably Crusch.

But Subaru, who knew Crusch's character, wanted to deny that suspicion head-on.

Crusch was noble. A woman of extraordinary caliber, capable of acting in accordance with sound judgment.

Even after losing her memories, even when her dignified aristocratic bearing had to be reconstructed, the inherent goodness and basic human decency that remained at her core had not been devoured by Gluttony.

There was no way someone like her would stain her hands with such an atrocity. It was impossible.

That was why Subaru wanted to hear a clear denial from her own lips.

He wanted her to say that, like Subaru and the others, she had noticed something amiss at the McMahon estate and rushed over, only to arrive too late. That, with regret and fury in her heart, she would track down Miklotov's killer and, together with them, make the culprit answer for their crimes.

That denial, from her lips—

"It seems you are the sort who engages in the most brazen shamelessness."

Despite Subaru's prayer, Crusch's expression remained unchanged as she fixed her gaze on Tiga and declared those words. In response, Tiga shrugged slightly.

"A harsh assessment, but I'll accept it. So, what's the answer?"

"——"

"Did you kill Lord Miklotov McMahon? Duchess Crusch Karsten?"

"—I did."

In response to Tiga's pressing question—this time unmistakably clear—a short reply came.

It affirmed the suspicion, betrayed Subaru's prayer-like hope. The worst possible answer. An involuntary rasping sound escaped Subaru's throat. That brief reply echoed again and again inside his skull.

As if to further fuel Subaru's violent agitation, Crusch inclined her chin ever so slightly.

"The personnel in this mansion, and Miklotov McMahon—I cut them down with my own hands. They were traitors, enemies of the kingdom."

"—Then the traitor here is you, Duchess!"

"Wha—!"

In that instant, Tiga roared, closing the distance in a single leap. He swung his curved blade—his shamshir—unleashing a flowing sword strike upon Crusch.

Not even giving Subaru time to say "Wait," that unhesitating first attack was parried by Crusch, who swung her own sword upward to deflect it. The clear ring of steel clashing against steel resounded through the office.

With that single note as the trigger, the shrieking of steel and scattering sparks erupted between the two.

"——"

The spark-scattering sword fight began, the spacious office becoming the stage upon which the two swordsmen displayed their skills.

What Crusch wielded was the sword art mastered by one who had earnestly dedicated herself to the blade. Whether in the battle against the White Whale before she lost her memories, or in the fight in Priestella after—the straightness of her sword's path never changed. Her sharp, flashing blade swept freely in every direction, leaping and bounding through the space.

On the other hand, Tiga's swordsmanship—low-stanced, spinning like a top—was unique.

Among those Subaru knew who excelled in swordsmanship—starting with Crusch, and including Julius, Wilhelm, and Cecils—aside from Reinhard, whose hand-blades and kicks left a stronger impression than the sword, all were swordsmen who had polished what could be called orthodox techniques.

If their swordsmanship was to be called the righteous path, Tiga's was the kind considered heretical. Not cutting an opponent down head-on, but mercilessly targeting hands and feet, mixing feints with real strikes, and prizing surprise attacks—the techniques of one who greedily sought victory.

"Sei—!"

With a fierce battle cry, he loosed a sword strike—his shamshir held in a reverse grip, tracing an unconventional arc as it closed in on his opponent. When Crusch leaned back to evade it, what followed was Tiga's long leg, lashing out like a whip.

The kicks woven between his sword strikes functioned as part of a series of movements almost like a dance, generating an unbroken wind of blades that assailed the opposing Crusch.

In response to that high-speed barrage, like a spinning top, Crusch—expression unchanged—maintained distance with swift, precise footwork, matching it with her single sword as she sought to seize the initiative in the dizzying exchange.

Perhaps her shoes were fitted with steel plates. Even against Crusch's sword meeting her kicks, Tiga's legs were not brought down. Against Crusch, who had mastered the quintessence of the orthodox style, the heretical swordsman showed a fighting form that yielded not a single step.

"Quite skilled. Does the Divine Dragon Church teach such ways of wielding a sword?"

"Unfortunately, it's self-taught. I've had bad habits with both hands and feet for a long time now!"

"A courageous wind. It seems that's no lie."

Crusch dodged Tiga's kick by a hair's breadth, then drove a vertical splitting blow straight down. Tiga caught it with his reverse-grip curved blade. Locked blade to blade, the two exchanged those words.

A seesaw battle of offense and defense. Both were fighters of superior ability, and so the advantage did not tip to either side, but—

"—Subaru! Pull yourself together, I suppose!"

"—Ah."

"Of course it's a shock. But you mustn't stand there dumbfounded, I suppose!"

With those words, Beatrice gave a little leap and slapped Subaru on the cheek, making his eyes snap wide.

At that popping impact, Subaru realized he had once again let his thoughts come to a halt, simply entranced by the battle between Tiga and Crusch. To Beatrice, he felt profound gratitude.

Certainly, he was in shock. Calmly reflecting on the situation, there was no way he could possibly maintain his composure. So now, before he confronted the situation coolly, there was something he had to do.

"My friend."

He saw Tiga, still pressing his blade against Crusch's, capture the interaction between Subaru and Beatrice at the edge of his vision, his lips forming that small, quiet utterance.

There was no object to that call, yet Subaru grasped Tiga's intent. What he sought was not aid, nor a sword at his back—

"—Outside."

They had to inform others of this situation, and stop Crusch's rampage.

Fortunately, right now in the royal capital, Subaru had many reliable allies. Garfiel and Wilhelm were here. Even Reinhard was here.

This was the noble district, so the mansions other than this McMahon estate should have knights and guards stationed at them as well. They had to call such forces, and apprehend Crusch.

And then—

"——"

And then... would they punish Crusch? As a traitor to the nation, for killing Miklotov.

"Stay here. I have yet to fully assess you."

Just as an inescapable hesitation crossed the corner of his mind, a blade of wind raced over Subaru's head.

"Whoa!" Reflexively, Subaru ducked his head. Grazing the tips of his bristling hair, the blade of wind rushed past behind him, struck the upper part of the room directly at his back, and destroyed the ceiling and wall near the entrance. The resulting collapse buried the room's doorway.

"The escape route...!"

The moment he realized it had been sealed, Subaru was forced to acknowledge, whether he wanted to or not, that his options had been cut down, leaving him in a state where his cards were woefully few.

"—!"

If the escape route was blocked, that meant the option of leaving this place was gone.

In this battlefield, there was no time to fumble through rubble and secure a path of retreat. His Yin Magic Minya lacked physical destructive power; he could not expect the force needed to blow away the blocked entrance all at once.

In other words, the only thing Subaru and the others could do was choose between standing by or intervening. And in this situation, standing by was absolutely the one option that could never be taken.

But intervening meant—

"—Fighting? Against Lady Crusch?"

It didn't make sense.

He couldn't understand this reality.

He couldn't accept what his eyes were seeing.

Because it was true, wasn't it? The person there was Crusch Karsten. Subaru respected her. Even if their camps were opposed in the Royal Selection, her virtue naturally deserved recognition. Crusch was noble, high-minded, an outstanding person of character who never neglected the effort and devotion required of a ruler. In terms of the qualities befitting a sovereign, she was surely the first person who had ever captivated Subaru. When they had defeated the White Whale and she had acknowledged that accomplishment from her own lips, how much that had meant in the life of Natsuki Subaru—no one could deny it, and he would let no one deny it.

He had prioritized his affection for Emilia. But even so, Subaru had truly thought so at that time.

That Crusch Karsten was, without a doubt, someone worthy of becoming the monarch of the Kingdom of Lugunica.

"—Please, just stop, Lady Crusch!"

And so, what Subaru chose at that moment had to be called the most spiritless card in his hand.

"——"

To Subaru's strained, desperate plea, Crusch—locked in fierce swordplay—did not respond. Her gaze was fixed squarely on Tiga, her opponent.

But Subaru believed she could not be ignoring him entirely. Believing that, he raised his voice.

"Lower your sword! Please! I don't know what we're supposed to do! I don't know, but I'll talk too! I'll talk, and we'll figure something out!"

Before he knew it, the hand opposite the one Beatrice held was gripping the black sphere hanging at his neck—gripping it tightly, desperately. With Al, he had been forced to suppress his will, leaving so many words that had to be exchanged unspoken.

Priscilla's death—which had been the trigger that drove Al to his rampage—had carved into Subaru the absolute weight of a loss from which what was lost could never be reclaimed.

To lose someone he knew, without even being able to speak with them. To Natsuki Subaru now, that was a fear far more terrible than his own death.

"I don't want it to end like this! Like this... not knowing a damn thing, I don't want to be torn apart from the people I care about!"

"Subaru..."

"Please! I'm begging you! I'm begging you, please... just stop!"

He could see Beatrice, gazing at his profile as he wrung out his voice, her eyes wavering as she bit her lip. How pained a face was he making as he pleaded?

Pathetic. Miserable. He was the partner of the Great Spirit Beatrice, and yet he couldn't put her wise and exceptional power to any use whatsoever—reduced to doing nothing but wailing like this.

"Please..."

Even knowing his own powerlessness, Subaru could not choose otherwise. If he couldn't run, then the thought of fighting Crusch—he could not bring himself to reach such a cut-and-dried conclusion.

There had been times before when a first encounter and a subsequent relationship had been drastically transformed.

But for the most part, that meant a negative impression had been changed into a positive one.

The reverse cases—there were only two: Todd and Al. Adding Crusch to that list... was something he simply could not endure.

"——"

Subaru's clumsy, faltering plea was so faint that it threatened to be drowned out by the intensifying clash of blades. It seemed as if it might be disregarded as lightly as Subaru's very presence in this battle, never even reaching Crusch's ears.

However—

"—It seems there is no lie in your words."

"—!"

At the sound of that murmur, as if comprehension had dawned, Subaru's eyes flew open in surprise.

Just then, with Tiga positioned between them, Subaru's and Crusch's gazes intersected over the heretical swordsman.

Meeting those amber eyes, Subaru felt a surge of hope that his words had reached her—

"In that case, I shall deal with you later."

—In that instant, with Crusch at its center, a violent wind rampaged through the room.

"Wha—!"

The moment his entire body felt the pressure, his feet left the floor. Subaru intuitively understood that it was a wind raised by Crusch, the result of her signature sword technique—One-Blade Hundred Foes—being unleashed not in a single focused direction, but in all directions at once.

Ordinarily, it would be a tremendous blade of wind concentrated and released in one direction. The wind pressure generated by scattering it in all directions instead mercilessly shook the office, already ravaged by battle, sending thick books, bundled documents, and even the corpse of the deceased sage elder flying into the air.

But Subaru's grasp of the situation only went that far.

"Gah—!"

His defenseless body, lifted off the ground, slammed into the ceiling with tremendous force, knocking the breath out of him. Reflexively, he had pulled his arms in, clutching Beatrice to his chest to protect her, but that was all.

"Subaru!" Beatrice screamed as Subaru held her tight, his body riding the wind, smashed against a wall, and then, unable to break his fall, was hurled unceremoniously to the floor.

"—gh"

Pressed by the pain that creaked through every inch of his body, the last breath escaped his lungs, and a sensation of coldness rapidly spread through the depths of his head. This is bad, he realized instinctively.

This was different from death. But his consciousness was flickering out. This was the price for prioritizing Beatrice's safety too much and neglecting himself. As if a hole had been opened in his head where he'd struck it, his consciousness was draining out through it. This is bad. Bad. Bad.

"No... good..."

If he lost consciousness now, who would throw words at Crusch?

Tiga didn't know. He didn't know Crusch's true character—that she was a genuinely kindhearted person. He misunderstood. He had to be stopped. Crusch was a good person. She would understand if they talked.

If they just talked, she would understand.

"Un-der...stand, so..."

So, please, don't shut your ears to my words. Praying that until the very, very last moment of his fading consciousness, Subaru's mind sank into the darkness with a splash.

It sank, and—






.........



.........



"—I did."

"——"

The moment his consciousness abruptly awakened, Subaru nearly lost his sense of balance.

His brain could not process the discrepancy between who he had been just moments before and who he was now—instantaneously switched—and that dissonance acted upon his limbs, sending him into a panic.

His feet were on the ground. Obviously, he was standing. He was standing. Of course. Was that really true? Had standing been the natural state of things? It had not been—which was why he now felt as if heaven and earth had been turned upside down—

"No."

What Subaru needed to focus on now was not the sensation of the world turned upside down. It was the cause of why he felt that heaven and earth had flipped.

And that cause went without saying. —He had gone back in time.

"——"

Subaru's knees nearly buckled. In his vision lay Miklotov's office in the McMahon estate, before it had been violently ravaged, and the corpse of Miklotov himself, cradled in the diagonally bisected national flag. Before that corpse stood a green-haired woman turning back in half-profile, and the back of a young man a half-step ahead of Subaru.

Crusch and Tiga, facing off. And this exchange had taken place—

"The personnel in this mansion, and Miklotov McMahon—I cut them down with my own hands. They were traitors, enemies of the kingdom."

It was that moment—the moment of that irreparable, fatal affirmation to the question.

"—Then the traitor here is you, Duchess!"

Before Subaru, whose blood ran cold with shock, Tiga let out a fierce battle cry and lunged in.

Tiga's sword dance began, his shamshir held in a reverse grip. And meeting it head-on was Crusch—a clash of swordsmen who walked the paths of heresy and orthodoxy.

Witnessing the battle raging in the office, with its chain of ringing steel and scattering sparks dancing wildly, Subaru felt vertigo wash over him for a reason entirely separate from the balance he had first fought to keep.

"No... that's impossible..."

He pressed a hand to his mouth, a strangled voice escaping him. The shock, unstoppable like blood seeping from a wound, seemed to pound against the inside of Subaru's head relentlessly.

—Return by Death. He had experienced Return by Death.

Just before his consciousness had cut out, what had blown Subaru away was the wind raised by Crusch. He had crashed into the walls and ceiling, been hurled to the floor, and knocked unconscious. —The fact that Subaru had then returned to this moment meant he had undergone Return by Death without ever waking up.

If Subaru's life had been taken while he was unconscious in that situation, the one who had done the deed was—

"—Subaru! Pull yourself together, I suppose!"

"—!"

"Of course it's a shock. But you mustn't stand there dumbfounded, I suppose!"

Beatrice pulled Subaru back to his senses—though he had frozen up for a different reason than before, her way of encouraging him was the same as last time. Her small palm struck his cheek. That pain and heat brought him back.

"Sorry, Beatrice."

Rubbing his struck cheek with the back of his hand, Subaru clenched his teeth until they creaked.

Even with a death sandwiched in between, the chaos and turmoil Subaru felt toward this situation did not lessen in the slightest. Yet even if he denied the events as lies and screamed that he would not accept them, the result would be the same.

Unable to muster the courage to intervene in the battle between Crusch and Tiga, Subaru would spout pathetic whining and die. —He would end up having his life snuffed out by Crusch's hand.

"No."

That must not be allowed to happen.

Crusch Karsten must not be made to take Natsuki Subaru's life. Because Subaru was weak, he could not be permitted to sully her nobility.

Crusch's hands must never, under any circumstances, be stained with Subaru's blood.

"Beako, lend me your help."

"—! You don't need to tell me, I suppose!"

Shock and panic had left the puzzle pieces of his reason scattered.

Yet from that scattered puzzle, Subaru grasped only the pieces whose shape he could still see clearly. He squeezed tightly the hand of his partner, who inspired nothing but absolute trust.

The battle continued before their eyes. The fight between Crusch and Tiga was evenly matched, a true back-and-forth struggle.

As someone who knew Crusch's strength, Subaru was amazed at the swordsmanship Tiga displayed, capable of rivaling hers. However, the situation was clearly disadvantageous, as Crusch possessed the decisive finishing blow capable of overwhelming him—One-Blade Hundred Foes.

Whether Tiga knew this or not, his choice of fighting at close range prevented Crusch from having the time to gather wind. But Subaru knew from bitter experience that even amid their swordplay, Crusch was stockpiling the wind that would overturn the situation.

Therefore—

"Don't rely on magic, my friend!"

"I couldn't use it in the first place, my friend!"

Whether reassuring or unreliable, a voice responded to his call. Subaru pulled up his arm, cradling Beatrice to his chest. The two of them, from a distance close enough that their cheeks might touch, fixed the same battle firmly in their sights—

"「—E・M・T!!」"

Their incantation overlapped. At the same moment, what spread through the office was an invisible field centered on Subaru and Beatrice—an original technique that could only be called anti-magic magic: EMT, which unraveled and neutralized constructed mana spell formulas, rendering them powerless.

Within this field, not only was using magic impossible, but even Meteors fueled by mana and magical implements would malfunction and become useless. According to Beatrice, the situation would change if an opponent adapted to that state of being inert and rewrote their spell formulas, but—

"At the very least, it creates more than enough opening to slap a startled Roswaal right across the face, I suppose!"

"Much less against someone seeing it for the first time... they won't know what the hell's going on!"

The effect of the deployed field swallowed up not only Subaru's group, but also the two locked in combat, dragging them under its influence.

As advertised, EMT had no effect on Tiga's tactics, since he didn't use magic. But the same could not be said for Crusch, who wove wind for her trump card.

Crusch, who had apparently braced herself for whatever was coming at Subaru and Beatrice's incantation, turned her lone eye toward them as the field effect of EMT impacted her. —Even before his Return by Death, Subaru had exchanged glances with Crusch.

At that time, his hopes had been betrayed, and Subaru had promptly lost his life, but—

"Surrender, Lady Crusch! Your path to victory is gone! You can't use One-Blade Hundred Foes! I won't let you! This is as far as it goes!"

Subaru plastered over his trembling voice with bravado, projecting an unyielding, powerful will.

He had learned that a weak-kneed, tearful voice, a heartbroken plea, could not change reality. What was needed here and now was a powerful kotodama—a spirit of words—strong enough to convey his will to his opponent.

He had to pour his soul into those words. He had to deceive his opponent—and himself—from the bottom of his heart.

He had to make her believe that Natsuki Subaru was an opponent not worth fighting.

"——"

Subaru's black eyes and Crusch's lone amber eye met squarely, measuring each other's resolve. In that interval, their swords clashed fiercely once, and the duel between Crusch and Tiga reached a pause.

Tiga, carrying his curved blade and standing half-turned in a stance shielding Subaru's group from behind, spoke.

"That's my friend's warning. To add to it, I'd guess that this friend is someone who was your friend too... how about you lend an ear?"

"Tiga..."

"Frankly speaking, I don't have the strength to unilaterally disarm and subdue you. If I try to restrain you, I'll need to be prepared to lose some flesh, and worst case, I'll lose my life. That's not something I want—nor those two behind me."

As he spoke, Tiga's gaze was fixed with unwavering vigilance on Crusch's every movement.

Perhaps, from his perspective, he had played along with Subaru with the feeling that even a single opening in Crusch might be a gain. Even so, what Tiga conveyed was unmistakably a breakdown of Subaru's intentions, making them easier to penetrate.

There was no way Tiga could know about what had happened between Subaru and Crusch.

If anything, if he knew about the Royal Selection, he ought to have the preconception that they were opposed camps. Yet Tiga had inferred from Subaru's brief words and actions here that his relationship with Crusch was by no means a simple adversarial one.

Subaru took advantage of Tiga's quick understanding. He would subdue Crusch.

Subdue her—and then, everything after that. From that point on, he would think about how to make everything go well.

"I am—"

"—Minya."

Just as Crusch began to speak, her lips parting while Subaru's heart felt like it was praying—Beatrice, cradled in Subaru's arms, raised her hand. A flash of violet light pierced the floor at Crusch's feet.

The purple arrow's strike, piercing near her toes, crystallized and cracked the office floor.

"Refrain from careless movements, I suppose. Even within EMT, Betty and Subaru are exceptions who can use magic. Do you think you can handle us while still contending with that flippant man?"

"I'm wounded by that 'flippant man' assessment, but you two can use your own magic, huh? That's cheating."

"That's the concept. But yeah, that's how it is."

At Beatrice's warning, pressing their advantage, Crusch's expression stiffened slightly—Subaru could tell. It was the reaction of someone who had not counted Subaru's group as a threat until now, now clearly and firmly including them as targets of caution.

It pained him to be seen that way. But if they were seen as a threat, that was a stroke of fortune. If the odds were against her, surely she would be able to make a sound judgment—

"I see."

With that, Crusch made a casual motion, swinging her sword and tracing a trajectory through the empty air.

It was a sudden, unexpected movement, but it was likely to confirm the effect of EMT. The blade of wind that should have been born was not. She gave a nod at the failure of One-Blade Hundred Foes to manifest.

And then—

"—Natsuki Subaru. What is it that you fear so greatly?"

Once again, Crusch's question flew at him.

But this time was different from the first. He did not let confusion swallow him. He grit his teeth and endured.

"The outcome of this battle? The safety of yourself and those beside you? The future of the kingdom? Or..."

"——"

"Or... my sanity?"

It was a question posed as if chewing over and imparting each word carefully.

To that question, Subaru used every muscle of his expression to maintain his current state. He crafted a face with all his might, so that not a single trace of his faint-heartedness or hesitation would reach her.

What had Crusch seen in that face of Subaru's, into which he had poured his entire soul?

She simply parted her thin lips briefly and murmured,

"I see."

Nothing more.

"Duchess, we've conveyed our demand. What is your answer?"

The wordless, momentary storm of emotion that had arisen between Subaru and Crusch—deliberately ignoring it, Tiga again questioned Crusch in a stern voice.

The tension filling his voice clearly announced that this was an ultimatum. The sword aura rising intensely once more—exposed to it, Crusch looked at Tiga.

"I understand your demand. Under these circumstances, with my wind sealed, I am at a disadvantage. Therefore, you insist that I surrender. That is your claim."

"—Yes. From here on, Beako and I will join in as well. Your chances of winning..."

"—It would be premature to assert that I have none, Natsuki Subaru."

With dignity, Crusch spoke over Subaru's bluff as if trampling it, and slowly raised her left hand—the one not holding her sword. Her slender fingers, sheathed in a white glove, touched the large eyepatch covering her left eye.

The eyepatch that largely concealed the left side of her face—the part she herself had said was not yet fully healed, even as it was being healed by the power of the sacrament used by Filore, against the influence of the Dragon's Blood. Watching those slender fingertips hook under it and strip it away as if tearing it off, Subaru's eyes widened.

Because—

"I would prefer not to show this appearance for long. —I am a woman, after all."

As Crusch spoke, her left eye—revealed bare once the eyepatch was removed—was shining gold, unlike her natural amber one. Slit pupils like a reptile's, and lines of light running beneath the eye like capillary blood vessels. Upon seeing it, a certain word flashed through Subaru's mind like a bolt of lightning.

"—Ah."

He didn't even know if it was correct.

But seeing Crusch's inhuman, golden eye now, he thought it.

"——"

—Crusch Karsten's left eye had become the Dragon's Eye.
